---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, LAST_REVIEWED, fmtDate, articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../data/site';
const schema = [articleSchema({ headline: 'How we rank recruitment tools', description: 'The criteria, weights and rules behind the rankings on BestRecruitmentTools.com.', path: '/how-we-rank/' }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'How we rank', path: '/how-we-rank/' }])];
---
<Base title={`How We Rank Recruitment Tools | ${SITE_NAME}`} description="The criteria, weights and rules behind every ranking on BestRecruitmentTools.com: who ranks, what counts, what is excluded, and how vendors can request corrections." path="/how-we-rank/" schema={schema} type="article">
  <div class="wrap narrow prose">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › How we rank</p>
    <h1>How we rank recruitment tools</h1>
    <p class="byline">Last revised {fmtDate(LAST_REVIEWED)}</p>
    <h2>The one rule</h2>
    <p>No vendor pays for placement, position or a review. We do not sell sponsored listings, “featured” slots or lead forms. If that ever changes it will be disclosed on this page and on every affected listing.</p>
    <h2>Why rankings instead of a list</h2>
    <p>Buyers ask “which one?”, not “what exists?”. A ranking forces us to make a call and explain it. We accept that the call is a judgement; the criteria below make the judgement inspectable.</p>
    <h2>Criteria and weights</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Weight</th><th>What we look at</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Work removed</td><td>30%</td><td>How many recruiter or manager hours the tool eliminates, not merely speeds up. Tools that complete a task (sourcing, screening, scheduling, note-taking) score above tools that assist with it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fit breadth</td><td>20%</td><td>How many kinds of team the tool serves well. A tool that is excellent for one use case and poor elsewhere can top a use-case list but not the overall ranking.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Value</td><td>20%</td><td>Outcome per dollar, including whether pricing is public, whether a trial or free tier exists, and how cost scales with seats and volume.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Depth and reliability</td><td>15%</td><td>Feature completeness within its category, data quality, uptime and support, judged from documentation, trials and practitioner reports.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Integration</td><td>15%</td><td>Whether it plugs into the systems that already hold candidate data (ATS, calendar, HRIS) without manual export.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h2>Ratings</h2>
    <p>The 1–5 rating on each review is the weighted result above, rounded to one decimal, for the teams the tool is designed for. A 4.0 agency ATS and a 4.0 enterprise CRM are not interchangeable; the use-case rankings and “best for” field carry that context.</p>
    <h2>Use-case rankings</h2>
    <p>Each “best for” guide re-weights the criteria for that situation. Startups weight value and work removed more heavily; enterprise weights integration, permissions and depth; high-volume weights speed and automation. The winner on each page is the tool we would recommend to a team in that situation with no further information.</p>
    <h2>What we exclude</h2>
    <ul><li>Tools with no verifiable product or customers.</li><li>Tools whose main function is outside recruiting (payroll-only, LMS) unless hiring depends on them, such as employer-of-record platforms for remote hiring.</li><li>“AI” tools where the AI does not perform a task a person used to do.</li></ul>
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>Vendor documentation and pricing pages, product trials where available, public practitioner communities, customer reviews on third-party sites (weighted lightly because of incentive programmes), and our own experience running recruiting processes. Pricing is checked at each review cycle and is approximate.</p>
    <h2>Review cycle and corrections</h2>
    <p>Rankings are reviewed quarterly; individual reviews are updated when a vendor materially changes product or pricing. Vendors and readers may request factual corrections via the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Corrections of fact are made promptly; requests to change ratings or positions are declined.</p>
    <h2>Disclosures</h2>
    <p>Links to vendor sites are plain links, not affiliate links. The site is funded by its publisher as an editorial project.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
